//? Title: Automatic Differentiation: Gradients from a Recorded Tape
//? Subtitle: The chain rule run backwards, vector-Jacobian and Jacobian-vector products, higher derivatives, the cost of a gradient and the error of finite differences, derived, checked by hand and applied to gradient descent on mtcars, using datamunge's nn::grad
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Training a neural network needs the gradient of a scalar loss with respect to many parameters,
to rounding error, at a small multiple of the cost of computing the loss. Finite differences
fail on both counts: they cost one or two evaluations per parameter, and they lose about a third
of the available digits. Reverse-mode automatic differentiation meets both needs. This report
derives it from the chain rule, as a backward sweep over a tape of the operations the forward
computation recorded. It then derives vector-Jacobian and Jacobian-vector products, higher
derivatives obtained by recording the backward sweep itself, and the step-size trade-off of
central differences. On \(y=x^3+2x\) at \(x=2\) the tape has three operations, and the adjoints of its
values are 1, 1, 1 and 14, as hand-worked. The third derivative is 6 at every point. The Jacobian of a three-output
function matches its hand form to six decimals, and stop_gradient halves \(\mathrm d(s\cdot s)/\mathrm ds\) from 6 to 3.
Central differences of \(\exp'(1)\) reach at best an error of \(7\times10^{-12}\) in float64 and \(2\times10^{-6}\) in float32, at steps
near the predicted \(h^*=(3\varepsilon)^{1/3}\). Autograd is exact in float64 and in error by \(8\times10^{-8}\) in float32. A gradient cost between
about 1.7 and 3.5 function evaluations for \(10^2\) to \(10^6\) inputs. On the mtcars data, gradient descent on the least-squares loss
converges at exactly the rate \((\kappa-1)/(\kappa+1)\) predicted by the Hessian, which autograd also supplies. That rate is 0.988 with the raw weight and
0.038 once the weight is centred, so the fit takes 1401 steps in one case and 6 in the other. The library gained
`nn::tape` and `nn::plot_tape`, to inspect and draw the recorded graph, and `nn::vjp`, `nn::jvp`,
`nn::jacobian`, `nn::hessian` and `nn::hvp`.
)

\toc

> The problem

A model with parameters \(\theta\in\mathbb R^n\) is trained by moving \(\theta\) against the gradient \(\nabla L(\theta)\) of a
scalar loss. Modern networks have \(n\) in the millions, and the gradient is needed at every step.
Writing derivatives by hand does not scale and is error-prone. Symbolic differentiation produces
expressions that grow quickly with the depth of the computation. Finite differences need about \(n\)
evaluations of \(L\) for one gradient, and they are inaccurate. Automatic differentiation (AD) computes
exact derivatives of the program that computes \(L\), at a cost proportional to the cost of \(L\) itself.

This report covers the reverse mode of AD as datamunge's `nn` library implements it. It replaces
the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/autodiff.cpp`, a port of
TensorFlow's `AddSymbolicGradients`. In TensorFlow 1 a graph was built first and gradients were added to it as more
graph nodes. In `nn`, as in PyTorch, each operation records itself on a *tape* as it runs (define-by-run), and
`nn::grad` walks the tape backwards. There are no placeholders or sessions: inputs are function
arguments, and variables are leaf tensors created with `requires_grad_()`.

> The method

>> The chain rule on a computational graph

Any program that computes \(y=f(x)\) from elementary operations can be written as a sequence of
intermediate values \(v_1,\dots,v_N\), where each \(v_k=\phi_k(v_{a(k)})\) applies an elementary function \(\phi_k\) to
earlier values \(v_{a(k)}\). The inputs are the first values and the output is the last. For \(y=x^3+2x\):

$$
v_1=x,\qquad v_2=v_1^3,\qquad v_3=2v_1,\qquad v_4=v_2+v_3=y.
$$
\alttext(v 1 equals x, v 2 equals v 1 cubed, v 3 equals 2 v 1, v 4 equals v 2 plus v 3, which is y.)

The values and their dependencies form a directed acyclic graph. The chain rule expresses the
derivative of the output with respect to any value as a sum over the values that use it. Define the *adjoint*
\(\bar v_k=\partial y/\partial v_k\). Then

$$
\bar v_N=1,\qquad \bar v_j=\sum_{k:\,j\in a(k)}\bar v_k\,\frac{\partial\phi_k}{\partial v_j},
$$
\alttext(The adjoint of the output v N is 1, and the adjoint of v j is the sum over every k that uses v j of the adjoint of v k times the partial derivative of phi k with respect to v j.)

since \(v_j\) can affect \(y\) only through the values computed from it. A value's adjoint depends only on the
adjoints of later values, so one sweep in reverse order of computation finds all of them. For the
example, \(\bar v_4=1\), \(\bar v_2=\bar v_4\cdot1=1\), \(\bar v_3=\bar v_4\cdot1=1\), and \(\bar v_1=\bar v_2\cdot3v_1^2+\bar v_3\cdot2=3x^2+2\), which is 14 at \(x=2\).

Each operation therefore needs one *backward function*: given the adjoint of its output, it returns the
adjoints contributed to its inputs, using values saved during the forward pass (here \(v_1\), for
\(3v_1^2\)). That is what the tape stores. Each node records the operation, edges to the nodes that produced
its inputs, and a closure for its backward function. A leaf tensor that requires a gradient ends
in an accumulator node, and an input that needs no gradient (a constant) has no edge.

>> Vector-Jacobian and Jacobian-vector products

For \(f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m\) with Jacobian \(J=\partial f/\partial x\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}\), start the sweep from a cotangent \(\bar y=v\in\mathbb R^m\) instead of 1.
By linearity every adjoint is then \(v^\top\partial y/\partial v_j\), and the input adjoint is

$$
\bar x=J^\top v,\quad\text{that is, the row vector }v^\top J .
$$
\alttext(The adjoint of x equals J transpose v, which is the row vector v transpose J.)

One backward pass gives one vector-Jacobian product (VJP), at a cost of a small constant times the cost of
\(f\). The gradient of a scalar loss is the case \(m=1\), \(v=1\). The full Jacobian takes \(m\) passes, one per output, with one-hot
seeds \(v=e_i\) that pick out row \(i\).

The forward mode carries a tangent \(\dot v_k=\sum_j(\partial\phi_k/\partial v_j)\dot v_j\) alongside each value instead, and yields the
Jacobian-vector product (JVP) \(Ju\) in one forward pass. A tape built for the reverse mode can give \(Ju\) too.
The map \(g(v)=J^\top v\) is linear in \(v\), so

$$
\nabla_v\langle g(v),u\rangle=\nabla_v\,v^\top Ju=Ju .
$$
\alttext(The gradient with respect to v of the inner product of g of v with u equals the gradient with respect to v of v transpose J u, which is J u.)

Recording the first backward pass as a function of a dummy \(v\), then differentiating it once more,
gives the JVP from two reverse sweeps (the "double-backward trick"). Reverse mode suits \(m\ll n\), which is
the training case. Forward mode suits \(n\ll m\), for example a sensitivity to one parameter.

>> The cost of a gradient

Each backward function costs at most a fixed multiple of its forward operation: for \(c=ab\), the two
adjoints \(\bar a=\bar c\,b\) and \(\bar b=\bar c\,a\) cost two multiplications against one. Summing over the tape gives the
*cheap-gradient principle* (Baur and Strassen, 1983; Griewank and Walther, 2008):

$$
\operatorname{cost}\bigl(f,\nabla f\bigr)\le\omega\,\operatorname{cost}(f),\qquad\omega\approx3\text{ to }5,
$$
\alttext(The cost of computing f together with its gradient is at most omega times the cost of f, with omega roughly 3 to 5.)

independent of the number of inputs. The price is memory. Every saved forward value is kept until the
backward sweep has used it.

>> Higher derivatives

The backward sweep is itself a computation built from differentiable operations, such as \(3v_1^2\cdot\bar v_2\). If it
is recorded on the tape (`create_graph`), the gradient it produces is a tensor with a history, and
`nn::grad` can differentiate it again. The Hessian \(\nabla^2f\) of a scalar \(f\) is the Jacobian of \(\nabla f\), at \(n\) reverse
sweeps over the recorded gradient. The Hessian-vector product needs one:

$$
\nabla^2f(x)\,v=\nabla_x\bigl\langle\nabla f(x),v\bigr\rangle,
$$
\alttext(The Hessian of f at x times v equals the gradient with respect to x of the inner product of the gradient of f at x with v.)

which holds because \(v\) does not depend on \(x\) (Pearlmutter, 1994). It costs about twice a gradient, without forming the
\(n\times n\) matrix.

>> Stopping a gradient

`stop_gradient(x)` returns the value of \(x\) with no history. The tape treats it as a constant, so
\(\partial\,\mathrm{stop}(x)/\partial x=0\) by fiat. For \(z=s\cdot\mathrm{stop}(s)\) the derivative is \(\mathrm{stop}(s)=s\), not \(2s\). This is used for targets that
must not be trained through, as in temporal-difference learning, for straight-through estimators, and
to cut a graph that would otherwise grow without bound.

>> Finite differences and why they are only a check

The central difference \(D_h=\bigl(f(x+h)-f(x-h)\bigr)/2h\) has, by Taylor's theorem, truncation error \(h^2f'''(\xi)/6\). In
floating point each evaluation of \(f\) carries a relative error of order \(\varepsilon\), the machine epsilon, and the
difference of two such values divided by \(2h\) carries an error of order \(\varepsilon\lvert f\rvert/h\). The total error is bounded by

$$
E(h)\approx\frac{h^2\lvert f'''\rvert}{6}+\frac{\varepsilon\lvert f\rvert}{h},\qquad
h^*=\Bigl(\frac{3\varepsilon\lvert f\rvert}{\lvert f'''\rvert}\Bigr)^{1/3},\qquad
E(h^*)=\tfrac32\,(\varepsilon\lvert f\rvert)^{2/3}\Bigl(\frac{\lvert f'''\rvert}{3}\Bigr)^{1/3},
$$
\alttext(The error E of h is about h squared times the absolute third derivative over 6, plus epsilon times the absolute value of f over h. It is smallest at h star, the cube root of 3 epsilon absolute f over absolute third derivative, where it equals three halves times epsilon absolute f to the power two thirds, times the cube root of the absolute third derivative over 3.)

from setting \(E'(h)=0\). The best achievable error is of order \(\varepsilon^{2/3}\), so about a third of the digits are lost.
Steps smaller than \(h^*\) make matters worse, not better. Reverse-mode AD has no step and loses only
the rounding of the operations themselves. Finite differences remain useful as an independent check on
backward functions, which is what `nn::gradcheck` does in float64.

>> In the library

The operations in `datamunge::nn` record themselves when any input requires gradients. The calls used here are:

- `nn::grad(outputs, inputs, seeds, options)` returns the VJPs \(\sum_i J_i^\top v_i\) without touching `Tensor::grad()`. Its
  options `retain_graph` and `create_graph` keep the tape for another sweep and record the sweep. Inputs may be
  intermediate tensors, which gives the adjoints of any value on the tape.
- `nn::tape(outputs, names)` (new) returns the recorded graph as plain data, in reverse recording order. It can be
  printed with `to_text()`, exported with `to_dot()`, or drawn with `nn::plot_tape()` (new).
- `nn::vjp`, `nn::jvp`, `nn::jacobian`, `nn::hessian` and `nn::hvp` (new, in `nn/derivatives.hpp`) take a
  function of one tensor. JVP uses the double-backward trick above, and the Hessian is the Jacobian of the recorded
  gradient.
- `nn::stop_gradient`, `nn::NoGradGuard` (no recording inside a scope), and `nn::gradcheck`/`nn::gradgradcheck`
  (first and second derivatives against central differences).

> Simple examples

>> One scalar function, by hand and on the tape

The first program records \(y=x^3+2x\) at \(x=2\), prints the tape and the adjoints of every value, then
differentiates three times. The gradient is recorded each time (`create_graph`), so it can be differentiated again.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/autodiff_tape.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // y = x^3 + 2x at x = 2, with the two intermediate results kept.
  auto x = nn::scalar(2.0, nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  auto u = nn::pow(x, 3.0);
  auto v = 2.0 * x;
  auto y = u + v;
  const auto t = nn::tape({y}, {{x, "x"}});
  std::print("```\n{}```\n\n", t.to_text());

  // Adjoints: dy/d(node) for every value on the tape.
  const auto adj = nn::grad(y, {y, u, v, x}, {.retain_graph = true});
  std::print("| value | forward | adjoint dy/d(value) |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const char* names[] = {"y = u + v", "u = x^3", "v = 2 x", "x"};
  const nn::Tensor vals[] = {y, u, v, x};
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n", names[i], vals[i].item(), adj[i].item());

  // Derivatives of the derivative: the backward pass recorded with create_graph.
  std::print("\n| x | y | dy/dx | expected 3x^2+2 | d2y/dx2 | expected 6x | d3y/dx3 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  nn::Tape backward_tape;
  for (double xv : {0.5, 1.0, 2.0}) {
    auto xx  = nn::scalar(xv, nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
    auto yy  = nn::pow(xx, 3.0) + 2.0 * xx;
    auto d1  = nn::grad(yy, {xx}, {.create_graph = true})[0];
    auto d2  = nn::grad(d1, {xx}, {.create_graph = true})[0];
    auto d3  = nn::grad(d2, {xx})[0];
    if (xv == 2.0) backward_tape = nn::tape({d1}, {{xx, "x"}});
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", xv, yy.item(), d1.item(),
               3 * xv * xv + 2, d2.item(), 6 * xv, d3.item());
  }
  std::print("\nThe recorded backward pass (dy/dx as a function of x) has {} ops:\n\n```\n{}```\n",
             backward_tape.op_count(), backward_tape.to_text());

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(nn::plot_tape(t, "Forward tape of y = x^3 + 2x"));
  fig.add(nn::plot_tape(backward_tape, "Recorded backward pass dy/dx"));
  fig.size(1100, 520).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/autodiff_tape.svg");
}
```
