// result_end

\caption(Left: the tape recorded for \(y=x^3+2x\). The output's node is at the top and the leaf \(x\) at the bottom. Each edge runs from an operation to the node that produced one of its inputs. The constant 2 in \(2x\) has no node. Right: the tape of the recorded backward pass, \(\mathrm dy/\mathrm dx=3x^2\cdot1+2\) as a function of \(x\).)
\alttext(Left: a diamond-shaped graph with Add_4 at the top joined to Mul_3 on the left and PowScalar_2 on the right, both joined to the blue leaf x at the bottom. Right: a vertical chain from Add_32 at the top through Mul_31, Mul_30 and PowScalar_29 down to the blue leaf x.)

The tape has three operations and one leaf, with the output's node first. `Mul_3 <- const x` shows that the
factor 2 entered as a constant with no edge. The adjoints match the hand sweep of the method section:
\(\bar y=\bar u=\bar v=1\), because addition passes its adjoint through unchanged, and \(\bar x=3x^2+2=14\). The first and second
derivatives match \(3x^2+2\) and \(6x\) at all three points, and the third derivative is 6 everywhere. The
right-hand tape shows what `create_graph` records. The backward function of \(x^3\) becomes the operations
\(x^2\) (`PowScalar`), times the incoming adjoint 1 (`Mul_30`), times 3 (`Mul_31`). The backward function of \(2x\)
contributes the constant 2, added by `Add_32`. That chain is the expression \(3x^2+2\), now differentiable in turn.

>> A Jacobian, its products, and a stopped gradient

For \(f(x)=(x_0x_1,\;x_0+x_1,\;\sin x_0)\) at \(x=(2,3)\), the Jacobian by hand is

$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}x_1&x_0\\1&1\\\cos x_0&0\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}3&2\\1&1\\\cos2&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad \cos2=-0.416147 .
$$
\alttext(J has rows x 1, x 0; 1, 1; and cos x 0, 0. At x equals 2, 3 this is 3, 2; 1, 1; and cos 2, 0, where cos 2 is minus 0.416147.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
  // f(x) = [x0 x1, x0 + x1, sin x0] at x = (2, 3).
  const auto f = [](const Tensor& x) {
    const Tensor x0 = nn::select(x, 0, 0), x1 = nn::select(x, 0, 1);
    return nn::stack({x0 * x1, x0 + x1, nn::sin(x0)});
  };
  const Tensor x = nn::tensor({2, 3}, {2}, F64);
  const auto J = nn::jacobian(f, x).to_vector<double>();
  std::print("| output | df/dx0 | df/dx1 | by hand |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  const char* rows[] = {"x0 x1", "x0 + x1", "sin x0"};
  const char* hand[] = {"(x1, x0) = (3, 2)", "(1, 1)", "(cos 2, 0)"};
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {} |\n", rows[i], J[2 * i], J[2 * i + 1], hand[i]);
  std::print("\ncos 2 = {:.6f}\n\n", std::cos(2.0));

  const Tensor v = nn::tensor({1, -1, 2}, {3}, F64), u = nn::tensor({1, 1}, {2}, F64);
  std::print("- v = (1, -1, 2): vjp gives vᵀJ = {}\n", nn::vjp(f, x, v).values_str());
  std::print("- u = (1, 1): jvp gives Ju = {}\n", nn::jvp(f, x, u).values_str());

  // stop_gradient: the second factor is frozen.
  auto s = nn::scalar(3.0, F64).requires_grad_();
  std::print("- d(s·stop(s))/ds at 3 = {:.0f}; d(s·s)/ds at 3 = {:.0f}\n",
             nn::grad(s * nn::stop_gradient(s), {s})[0].item(), nn::grad(s * s, {s})[0].item());
  const auto ts = nn::tape({s * nn::stop_gradient(s)}, {{s, "s"}});
  std::print("\n```\n{}```\n", ts.to_text());
}
```
